===Alternative forms===
* When this character is used as a top component, the form appears differently depending on the script used:
** In Japanese {{w|kanji}}, Korean {{w|hanja}} and Vietnamese [[Nôm]], the final {{l|mul|㇏}} stroke at the bottom right of the component protrudes from the second horizontal stroke of {{l|mul|三}} and intersects with the third horizontal stroke, which is also the historical form found in the {{w|Kangxi dictionary}}.
** In modern Chinese scripts, the final {{l|mul|㇏}} stroke at the bottom right of the component protrudes from the third horizontal stroke of {{l|mul|三}} and does not intersect with any of the lines.
** Note that {{l|mul|丿}} and {{l|mul|㇏}} are written apart and do not overlap or intersect one another unlike the bottom portion of {{l|mul|夫}}.
* A similar character {{l|mul|⿻㇒夫}} (the uppermost horizontal stroke is replaced by curved {{l|mul|㇒}}) can be found in the historical {{lang|zh|《古文四聲韻》}}.

===Han character===
{{Han char|rn=37|rad=大|as=02|sn=5|four=|canj=QK|ids=⿸⿻三丿㇏(GV),⿻⿻三丿㇏(J)}}

====Glyph origin====
The character represents various things according to the single character, hence all the characters are unrelated each other. The apparent relations comes from the stylization of the top component.

For instance:
* in the character [[泰]], it represents a man [[大]] (phonetic component) with a pair of hands under him;
* in the character [[秦]] and [[舂]], it represents a pair of hands holding a pestle [[午]];
* in the character [[春]], it represents grass and a sprout [[屯]];
* in the character [[奏]], it probably represents two hands around a tree (drawn as a sprout [[屮]]).

